{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgYY8q9Thbirbt8445dh2", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"hypotheses\":[{\"mechanism\":\"larger micro-batch, no gradient accumulation (2 x 8 GPUs x 1 = global batch 16)\",\"change\":{\"per_device_train_batch_size\":2,\"gradient_accumulation_steps\":1},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":22.0,\"uncertainty_pct\":15.0,\"p_feasible\":0.85,\"change_class\":\"numerics\",\"rationale\":\"The model (hidden 2816, 30 layers, vocab 262k, 25.8B params) looks like a Gemma-4 26B-A4B-style MoE running through HF eager experts. An ideal update for about 4B active params x 8k tokens per GPU is roughly 0.3-0.4 s, but the measured update is 3.38 s, and compute is 92% of it. So compute is dominated by per-micro-batch fixed costs: expert-loop kernel launches, small GEMMs, and FSDP all-gather and reduce-scatter per micro-batch. Running one micro-batch of 2 instead of two of 1 pays those costs once and doubles GEMM M per expert. The global batch stays 16. The two knobs are needed together to keep it.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"spends\",\"gib\":20.0},\"variance\":false},{\"mechanism\":\"partial activation recompute: checkpoint every 2nd layer\",\"change\":{\"gradient_checkpointing_kwargs\":\"{\\\"every_n_layers\\\": 2}\"},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":9.0,\"uncertainty_pct\":6.0,\"p_feasible\":0.8,\"change_class\":\"execution\",\"rationale\":\"Full recompute replays every layer's forward, including the overhead-heavy MoE expert loop. That costs about 20-25% (rule H7: full recompute -21%). Checkpointing only half the layers removes about half of that tax. It is a lower-memory alternative to the queued gradient_checkpointing=false. Estimated cost is about 0.6-0.7 GiB per un-checkpointed layer at seq 4096, which is well within the 67 GiB margin.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"spends\",\"gib\":10.0},\"variance\":false},{\"mechanism\":\"partial activation recompute: checkpoint every 4th layer\",\"change\":{\"gradient_checkpointing_kwargs\":\"{\\\"every_n_layers\\\": 4}\"},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":14.0,\"uncertainty_pct\":8.0,\"p_feasible\":0.8,\"change_class\":\"execution\",\"rationale\":\"Same mechanism as every_n_layers 2, but about 3/4 of the layers skip recompute. That recovers most of the roughly 20% recompute tax at about 16 GiB of extra activations, which fits the 67 GiB margin.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"spends\",\"gib\":16.0},\"variance\":false},{\"mechanism\":\"grouped-GEMM MoE experts (transformers experts_implementation=grouped_mm) instead of the per-expert Python loop\",\"change\":{},\"patch\":\"append to train_patches.py: wrap PreTrainedModel.from_pretrained so it requests experts_implementation='grouped_mm'; fall back to the original call on TypeError (older transformers without the kwarg)\",\"edits\":[{\"file\":\"train_patches.py\",\"op\":\"append\",\"text\":\"\\n# InfraRSI: request grouped-GEMM MoE experts when transformers supports it\\ntry:\\n    from transformers import modeling_utils as _isx_mu\\n    _isx_orig_fp = _isx_mu.PreTrainedModel.from_pretrained.__func__\\n    def _isx_fp(cls, *args, **kwargs):\\n        if 'experts_implementation' in kwargs:\\n            return _isx_orig_fp(cls, *args, **kwargs)\\n        try:\\n            return _isx_orig_fp(cls, *args, experts_implementation='grouped_mm', **kwargs)\\n        except TypeError:\\n            return _isx_orig_fp(cls, *args, **kwargs)\\n    _isx_mu.PreTrainedModel.from_pretrained = classmethod(_isx_fp)\\nexcept Exception as _isx_e:\\n    print('[infrarsi] grouped_mm experts patch not applied:', _isx_e)\\n\"}],\"predicted_gain_pct\":20.0,\"uncertainty_pct\":25.0,\"p_feasible\":0.6,\"change_class\":\"numerics\",\"rationale\":\"An update about 10x slower than its FLOPs and a 92% compute share point to HF eager MoE: a loop over many experts with tiny GEMMs, plus index and scatter glue that is repeated in recompute and backward. Rule H6 also says HF MoE is overhead-bound. A single grouped GEMM per projection removes most launches and raises GEMM efficiency. The gain is 0 if this transformers version already defaults to grouped_mm or lacks the option. The patch then falls back harmlessly. Summation order changes slightly, so the class is numerics.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"neutral\",\"gib\":0.0},\"variance\":false,\"warmup_updates\":2},{\"mechanism\":\"FSDP2 without reshard after forward (no re-all-gather of parameters in backward)\",\"change\":{\"fsdp_config\":\"fsdp_v2_noreshard.json\"},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":3.0,\"uncertainty_pct\":4.0,\"p_feasible\":0.7,\"change_class\":\"execution\",\"rationale\":\"Keeping unsharded bf16 parameters from forward to backward drops one all-gather per layer per micro-batch. With recompute it also removes the extra gather during recompute. The comm phase is only 2.1% exposed, but the gathers also delay compute. Cost is about 48 GiB of full bf16 params minus the 6 GiB shard, so about +42 GiB against a 67 GiB margin. That fits, but with limited headroom given the +-25% accounting.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"spends\",\"gib\":42.0},\"variance\":false},{\"mechanism\":\"expandable_segments allocator\",\"change\":{\"PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF\":\"expandable_segments:True\"},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":1.5,\"uncertainty_pct\":2.0,\"p_feasible\":0.95,\"change_class\":\"execution\",\"rationale\":\"MoE routing gives variable token counts per expert every step. Together with FSDP gather and free cycles and the 262k-vocab logits, this fragments the caching allocator and can trigger cudaMalloc/free and retries. Expandable segments reduce that cost. They act on every phase.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"frees\",\"gib\":3.0},\"variance\":false},{\"mechanism\":\"TF32 for remaining fp32 matmuls (router / softcap / fp32 upcasts)\",\"change\":{\"tf32\":true},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":1.0,\"uncertainty_pct\":1.5,\"p_feasible\":0.98,\"change_class\":\"numerics\",\"rationale\":\"The phase targets name tf32 for compute. Most GEMMs are bf16, so only fp32 ops are affected, such as the router gate and any fp32 upcast matmuls. The gain is small but cheap.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"neutral\",\"gib\":0.0},\"variance\":false},{\"mechanism\":\"disable NVLS (NVSwitch SHARP) collectives\",\"change\":{\"NCCL_NVLS_ENABLE\":\"0\"},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":0.4,\"uncertainty_pct\":1.0,\"p_feasible\":0.97,\"change_class\":\"execution\",\"rationale\":\"NVLS reserves SM-free multicast buffers and can slow small or overlapped reduce-scatter and all-gather compared with ring/tree. It also frees some NCCL memory. The comm phase is only 71 ms, so the effect is bounded at about 2%.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"frees\",\"gib\":1.0},\"variance\":false},{\"mechanism\":\"more dataloader workers\",\"change\":{\"dataloader_num_workers\":4},\"patch\":null,\"edits\":[],\"predicted_gain_pct\":0.3,\"uncertainty_pct\":0.8,\"p_feasible\":0.98,\"change_class\":\"execution\",\"rationale\":\"The residual of 148 ms (4.4%) may include host-side batch fetch and collation for 4k-token batches. Extra workers can only shave the input part of the residual.\",\"memory\":{\"direction\":\"neutral\",\"gib\":0.0},\"variance\":false}]}"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 9702, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 9702}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "79abddc9-377f-4628-930e-31b8389d465c", "uuid": "714cf8ae-51d8-4b47-a93f-739160bccdc0", "timestamp": "2026-10-30T13:57:45.587Z", "req