{"hypotheses":[
{"mechanism":"Drop activation recompute now that Liger's fused linear cross-entropy no longer creates the full fp32 logits. The 262144-vocab logits at mbs2 x 4096 are about 8 GiB in fp32 plus the bf16 copy and its gradient. Without recompute, the second forward pass per layer goes away.","change":{"gradient_checkpointing":false},"patch":"","edits":[],"predicted_gain_pct":6.0,"uncertainty_pct":8.0,"p_feasible":0.5,"change_class":"execution","memory":{"direction":"spends","gib":45.0},"rationale":"c10 (gradient checkpointing off, mbs2, no Liger) ran out of memory. The failed allocation was exactly 8.00 GiB, which is the fp32 logits tensor (2x4096x262144x4 B), and it was short by 6.89 GiB. On c12, Liger's fused linear cross-entropy never materializes that tensor or its gradient, and Liger's fused RMSNorm and GeGLU save some activations. That removes more than the 6.89 GiB shortfall, so this is a re-base of a move that is not marked unrunnable. On gain: the H7 rule puts the full-recompute cost at about 21%. Here every_n 2 and 3 measured about 0, which suggests compute is limited by overhead or synchronization rather than raw FLOPs. I therefore expect only part of that 21%. The prediction is wide; this is still the largest single compute-phase lever left."},
{"mechanism":"Selective recompute on the new base: checkpoint every 2nd layer at mbs2 with Liger. This removes about half the recompute forward, and Liger's freed logits memory pays for the extra activations.","change":{"gradient_checkpointing_kwargs":"{\"every_n_layers\": 2}"},"patch":"","edits":[],"predicted_gain_pct":1.5,"uncertainty_pct":3.0,"p_feasible":0.8,"change_class":"execution","memory":{"direction":"spends","gib":25.0},"rationale":"This move measured -0.25% on c0, but c0 ran mbs1 with gas2, where per-micro-batch overhead hid compute savings. At mbs2 and gas1 the GEMMs are larger, so recompute is a larger part of the 3.1 s compute phase. The memory margin is about 67 GiB by accounting, plus the Liger savings, so it should fit. If gradient checkpointing off fits, this move is dominated by it; it serves as the fallback if that one runs out of memory."},
{"mechanism":"Grouped-GEMM MoE experts: ask transformers to use its grouped_mm experts implementation instead of the eager per-expert Python loop. The eager loop launches small GEMMs and gather/scatter kernels per expert and often forces host syncs (nonzero/tolist).","change":{},"patch":"# --- InfraRSI: grouped_mm MoE experts (append to train_patches.py; must be imported before the model is loaded) ---\ntry:\n    import transformers as _tf\n    _orig_from_pretrained = _tf.PreTrainedModel.from_pretrained.__func__\n    def _from_pretrained_grouped(cls, *args, **kwargs):\n        kwargs.setdefault(\"experts_implementation\", \"grouped_mm\")\n        try:\n            model = _orig_from_pretrained(cls, *args, **kwargs)\n        except (TypeError, ValueError):\n            kwargs.pop(\"experts_implementation\", None)\n            model = _orig_from_pretrained(cls, *args, **kwargs)\n        try:\n            if hasattr(model, \"set_experts_implementation\"):\n                model.set_experts_implementation(\"grouped_mm\")\n            elif getattr(model, \"config\", None) is not None:\n                model.config._experts_implementation = \"grouped_mm\"\n                for _sub in (getattr(model.config, \"text_config\", None),):\n                    if _sub is not None:\n                        _sub._experts_implementation = \"grouped_mm\"\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        return model\n    _tf.PreTrainedModel.from_pretrained = classmethod(_from_pretrained_grouped)\nexcept Exception:\n    pass\n","edits":[{"file":"train_patches.py","op":"append","content":"(see patch)"}],"predicted_gain_pct":10.0,"uncertainty_pct":15.0,"p_feasible":0.4,"change_class":"numerics","memory":{"direction":"neutral","gib":0.0},"rationale":"The model has 25.81B parameters, but hidden 2816 with 30 layers is far too small for a dense model of that size, so it is an MoE. The accounting flags it as MoE as well. Rule H6 says HF-implemented MoE is overhead-bound. The measurements